{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":1,"author":{"id":"b3cab00338425c0da6d741f037644050ffaa3e47"},"timestamp":1580205278,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDU6SXNzdWU1NTYwOTY1NDc=","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/4098","origin":"github"},"title":"Observations on inconsistent handling of relative paths across python commands","message":"When I updated command examples to consistently include ``dataset=`` for the Python API following https://github.com/datalad/datalad/pull/4091#discussion_r371304348, I noticed inconsistent interpretations of ``path=\u003csome/path/\u003e`` between Python functions. Some interpret it as relative to ``dataset=\u003csome/path\u003e``, and some not.\n\nI'm aware it is a known issue, but I wanted to write down  which commands treat paths in which way when the dataset specification is a path (and not a dataset instance).\n\n\nI had an ipython instance in ``/tmp`` and used the Python API to work with a dataset `/tmp/super`, created like this in ``/tmp``:\n```\nfrom datalad.api import create, Dataset\n# create dataset (with path)\ncreate(path=\"super\")\n# also have a Dataset instance for it\nds = Dataset('super') \n```\nI checked the commands `create`,`save`,`unlock`,`download_url`, `get`, `clone`, `run`, `rerun`, `uninstall`, `diff`,`run-procedure`, `remove`, `drop`, `publish`, `siblings` (the latter four are the odd ones out)\n\n\n#### Behavior when dataset is a path (not dataset instance)\n\nPaths are **not** interpreted as relative to the dataset root in the following commands :\n- ``create`` (i.e., subdataset creation fails here: ``create(dataset='super', path='sub')``)\n- ``save`` (i.e., saving a file `file1` in ``super`` fails here: ``save(dataset='super', path='file1'``)\n- ``unlock`` (i.e, unlocking a file `file1` in `super` fails here: ``unlock(dataset='super', path='file1')``\n- ``download_url`` (i.e, downloading a file and saving it as `downloadfile` in `super` fails here: ``download_url(dataset='super', urls='https://raw.githubusercontent.com/datalad/datalad.org/7e8e39b1f08d0a54ab521586f27ee918b4441d69/content/asciicast/seamless_nested_repos.sh', message=\"did yx\", path='downloadfile')``\n- ``get`` (i.e., reobtaining a downloaded file ``downloadfile`` from `super` fails here: ``get(dataset='super', path='downloadfile')``\n- ``clone``, (i.e., installing a subdataset `podcasts` into super fails here: ``clone(dataset='super', source='https://github.com/datalad-datasets/longnow-podcasts.git', path='podcasts')``\n- ``run``, (i.e, running `mv file1 file_1` fails here: `run(dataset='super', cmd='mv file1 file_1', outputs='file1')`\n- ``rerun`` simply says ``No lines in history match specification`` after `rerun(dataset='super')` (see details at the end of this issue)\n- `uninstall` (i.e., uninstalling ``podcasts`` fails here: ``uninstall(dataset='super', path='podcasts')``)\n- ``diff`` (i.e., getting the diff on ``anotherfile`` in ``super`` fails here: `diff(dataset='super', path='anotherfile')`\n\n- a bit unrelated, but ``run-procedure`` works regardless of how I specify the dataset: ``run_procedure(dataset='super', spec='cfg_text2git')``\n\nPaths are interpreted as **relative** to the dataset root (specified as a string with ``dataset=``) in following commands:\n- ``remove`` (i.e., removing a subdataset `podcasts` from `super` works here: ``remove(dataset='super', path='podcasts')``\n- ``drop`` (i.e., dropping a file ``downloadfile`` from `super` works here: ``drop(dataset='super', path='downloadfile')``)\n- ``siblings``. I created ``/tmp/super_sibling`` (my dataset is ``/tmp/super``, ipython runs in ``/tmp``). Adding a sibling with a relative path works here: \n```\nsiblings(action='add', dataset='super', name='sibling-local', url='../super_sibling')                                                                     \n.: sibling-local(+) [../super_sibling (git)]\nOut[95]: \n[{'action': 'add-sibling',\n  'path': '/tmp/super',\n  'type': 'sibling',\n  'name': 'sibling-local',\n  'url': '../super_sibling',\n  'fetch': '+refs/heads/*:refs/remotes/sibling-local/*',\n  'annex-uuid': '172fb4d5-9b59-4f9e-ac05-66d65d161ce2',\n  'annex-ignore': 'false',\n  'annex-description': 'sibling-local',\n  'status': 'ok',\n  'refds': '/tmp/super'}]\n\n```\nIf I add a sibling with a path relative to the location my ipython instance runs from, it gets it wrong (sibling is wrongly under `super/super_sibling2` after this):\n```\nsiblings(action='add', dataset='super', name='sibling-local2', url='super_sibling2')                                                                      \n[INFO   ] Failed to enable annex remote sibling-local2, could be a pure git or not accessible \n[WARNING] Failed to determine if sibling-local2 carries annex. \n.: sibling-local2(-) [super_sibling2 (git)]\nOut[97]: \n[{'action': 'add-sibling',\n  'path': '/tmp/super',\n  'type': 'sibling',\n  'name': 'sibling-local2',\n  'url': 'super_sibling2',\n  'fetch': '+refs/heads/*:refs/remotes/sibling-local2/*',\n  'annex-ignore': True,\n  'status': 'ok',\n  'refds': '/tmp/super'}]\n\n```\n- ``publish`` (i.e., this works: ``publish(dataset='super', path='file_1', to='sibling-local')``\n\n________________________________________________________\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\u003csummary\u003eJust for completeness: if dataset= gets a dataset instance or if the command is used as a dataset method, paths are interpreted as relative\u003c/summary\u003e\n_______________________________________________________\n\n#### Behavior when dataset is dataset instance\n\nPaths are interpreted as **relative** to the dataset root (specified as a dataset instance with ``dataset=``) in all commands I have tried:\n- ``create(dataset=ds, path='sub')``\n- ``save(dataset=ds, path='file1')``\n- ``unlock(dataset=ds, path='file1')``\n- ``download_url(dataset=ds, urls='https://raw.githubusercontent.com/datalad/datalad.org/7e8e39b1f08d0a54ab521586f27ee918b4441d69/content/asciicast/seamless_nested_repos.sh', message=\"did yx\", path='downloadfile')``\n- ``clone(dataset=ds, source='https://github.com/datalad-datasets/longnow-podcasts.git', path='podcasts')``\n-  ``drop(dataset=ds, path='downloadfile')``\n- ``remove(dataset=ds, path='sub')`` \n- ``get(dataset=ds, path='downloadfile')``\n-`` run(dataset=ds, cmd='mv file1 file_1', outputs='file1') ``\n- ``rerun`` simply says ``No lines in history match specification`` after `rerun(dataset=ds)` (see details at the end of this issue)\n- ``uninstall(dataset=ds, path='podcasts')``\n- ``siblings(action='add', dataset=ds, name='sibling-local', url='../super_sibling')``\n- ``publish(dataset=ds', path='file_1', to='sibling-local')``\n- `diff(dataset=ds, path='anotherfile') `\n\n_______________________________________________________\n#### Behavior when using a dataset method instead of function\n\nPaths are interpreted as **relative** to the dataset root when used as a dataset method in all commands I have tried:\n- ``ds.create(path='sub')``\n- ``ds.save(path='file1')``\n- ``ds.unlock(path='file1')``\n- ``ds.download_url(urls='https://raw.githubusercontent.com/datalad/datalad.org/7e8e39b1f08d0a54ab521586f27ee918b4441d69/content/asciicast/seamless_nested_repos.sh', message=\"did yx\", path='downloadfile')``\n- ``drop(dataset=ds, path='downloadfile')``\n- ``ds.clone(source='https://github.com/datalad-datasets/longnow-podcasts.git', path='podcasts')``\n- `ds.remove(path='sub')`\n- ``ds.get(path='downloadfile')``\n- ``ds.run(cmd='mv file1 file_1', outputs='file1')``\n- ``ds.siblings(action='add', name='sibling-local', url='../super_sibling')``\n- ``ds.publish(path='file_1', to='sibling-local')``\n- ``ds.diff(path='anotherfile') ``\n\n\na bit unrelated, but ``run-procedure`` works regardless of how I specify the dataset: ``run_procedure(dataset=ds, spec='cfg_text2git')`` / ``ds.run_procedure(spec='cfg_text2git')``\n \n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n________________________________________________________\n\nI think I found an unrelated bug when I wanted to rerun a previous ``run`` command. It does not work when I pass the dataset as a string or -- interestingly -- also a dataset instance, but works if I use the command as a dataset method. When it \"fails\", the it just returns  ``No lines in history match specification`` instead of (what I think should be) complaining about a non-existent dataset.\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\nThis \"fails\" with what I find a misleading message:\n```\nIn [69]: rerun(dataset='super')                                                       \nNo lines in history match specification\n\nIn [70]: rerun(dataset=ds)                                                            \nNo lines in history match specification\n```\n\nworks as dataset method:\n\n```\nds.rerun()                                                                   \n[INFO   ] == Command start (output follows) ===== \n[...]\n```\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\nLet me know whether this is a feature or if I should open a new issue about it.","files":null}]}